#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125390} :activity/id #uuid "6257a384-69ce-502a-8a3f-5b199a93b00a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125780 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ invertible matrix $A.$ Remember that if $A$ is invertible, then the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has only the trivial solution $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's extract all possible information from this fact using the vector spaces related to the matrix, namely, the *column space* and the *null space*.\n\n- The fact that the unique solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ tells us that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},\n$$\n\n or alternatively, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{nullity}(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Furthermore, if $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=0$ then $A$ has no *non-pivot* columns, or equivalently, all the columns of $A$ are *pivot columns*. So \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n- Finally, since $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its basis has $n$ linearly independent vectors, we can conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nAll the results mentioned above can be summarized in the following theorem, called the **invertible matrix theorem**:\n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n![](images/7e/7ef5d72a30dd7f79f6182c4d78d705a7771f9f90ff0bb50f6d539614cb1e5b4d.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "555ca00c-d37e-5c67-a658-69b087390920" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3522 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4620379b-2eed-5dd7-bf3a-9edf0b4aea79" :step/math-academy-id 19988 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124320 :question/id #uuid "266a9a4e-cb11-5e4a-a2ac-c2e734966372" :question/math-academy-id "e-5424"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446375}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "77230797-e80e-5644-8b1e-9e3722a0b66d" :step/math-academy-id 19991 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124433 :question/id #uuid "c1120305-c8cb-5f57-97b1-9b31dc059b4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9961"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446380}] :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7ebf299f-89ad-55ae-8fe6-d0af3bc56dfd" :step/math-academy-id 37508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124434 :question/id #uuid "fff1daf8-5a1f-54c9-90d7-87710ca00d23" :question/math-academy-id "e-9962"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446379}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "debf1d2f-c0e6-5c61-b195-a95710e2c35e" :step/math-academy-id 37509 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125391}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124685}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435679} :activity/id #uuid "26e5acff-8db9-5e5d-9122-36baf139baac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "293235d4-a2f2-5777-a667-1540f003765f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435670} {:answer/id #uuid "46090913-fe8a-53d8-a2e2-1edec9407dcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435671} {:answer/id #uuid "596e4b3c-1fd0-573a-82a9-d1617135d478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435672} {:answer/id #uuid "75acd049-7010-5a98-8813-f29e53e8c979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435673} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2003c1-be5a-5ec0-8d3f-914e8ee86a4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435674} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e9f16a7-f4b2-5dc6-b4b7-1d7e51e453fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ea4581af-a083-51da-803f-0f83b3ed2ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-79349" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and  $det(A)=1?$ \n\nI. $A$ is not invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=4$\n\nIII. $A$ has $5$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{rank}(A)=5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=5$,  it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5$ which, in turn, means that  $A$ has $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "42d8e8b3-7048-5f94-884b-ae373fb59042" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435678}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188d8c1d-5896-5f10-81c9-bd96ad0517aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269475} {:answer/id #uuid "52e6f0c7-0a62-59e3-a5cb-80e9b7f54638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270005} {:answer/id #uuid "54208619-14dc-516d-b1aa-20ed09a0bb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270018} {:answer/id #uuid "77353fd7-ee88-5664-aec8-e00589b061d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270324} {:answer/id #uuid "af2612fc-39ba-5012-92ba-66032676ab46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186270827} :answer-field/id #uuid "94b73dce-7f8c-597e-ae8e-4e20e2b2ff1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8dad0d98-37c5-5af9-9c0a-4f0fabec2bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141200" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9a467517-2fb7-54c3-b455-e47e7392ef56"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef04a305-48e9-5100-9ee7-37ef95dd031d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121779} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ebd6d4-ef22-55d1-b2fd-1d2f280fa42a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121780} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1a9ac3-b9a3-59ca-891f-c8d93f27fd0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121781} {:answer/id #uuid "62f0ec41-6c5f-51cb-9439-22250619fa40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121782} {:answer/id #uuid "730f0ca8-ac97-51bf-ae0d-7facf7eedff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121782} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d952797-9510-50c2-afac-7e03150f3737" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a5b63a26-4713-5ecd-9d64-befaccc0bd77" :question/math-academy-id "q-34834" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?\n$$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n\nII. $A$ is not invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(A)=5$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "d2193342-5bc1-5a6f-99d1-42bf1fad6047" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435677}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435669}]}
